{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls -la results; ls results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1; cat results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/program_spec.toml; sed -n '1,220p' results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/program.py\"","aggregated_output":"lrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent 16 Nov 17 19:56 results -> ../investigation\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\nschema_version = 1\ncode_root = \"src\"\n\n[entrypoint]\npath = \"program.py\"\nlanguage = \"python\"\ntype = \"script\"\n\n[copy]\nexclude = [\n  \"**/__pycache__/**\",\n  \"**/*.pyc\",\n  \".venv/**\",\n  \"node_modules/**\",\n  \".git/**\",\n  \".mypy_cache/**\",\n  \".pytest_cache/**\",\n  \".ruff_cache/**\",\n  \".tox/**\",\n  \".nox/**\",\n  \"dist/**\",\n  \"build/**\",\n]\ninclude = [\n]\n\n[integration]\nkind = \"generic\"\nnotes = \"\"\n\"\"\"CPU-only frozen-predictor mean interval; finite JSON RPC v1.\n\nAll request/response fields below are mandatory; extra fields are forbidden.\nreset {op:\"reset\",seed:int} -> {ok:true}: clears buffers, fit and RNG. Accept\nany nonnegative signed-64-bit seed; seeds do not identify workloads or planes.\nupdate/start {op:\"update\",phase:\"start\",n:int,N:int,alpha:0.05,\n label_kind:\"bernoulli\"|\"real\",dtype:\"<f8\",encoding:\"base64\",shape:[2*n+N],\n total_bytes:8*(2*n+N),chunks:ceil(total_bytes/1048576)} ->\n {ok:true,transfer_id:str}. n,N>=8; logical input <=8 MiB, at most 8 chunks.\nupdate/chunk {op:\"update\",phase:\"chunk\",transfer_id:str,index:int,data:str}\n -> {ok:true}. data is standard base64; indices must be sequential from zero.\n Decoded chunks are 1 MiB except the last. The ID is an opaque memory token,\n 1..64 ASCII alphanumeric/underscore characters, never a filename or path.\nupdate/commit {op:\"update\",phase:\"commit\",transfer_id:str} -> {ok:true}.\n Assembled little-endian float64 array contains y[n], p[n], u[N]: labeled\n outcomes, their frozen predictions, then unlabeled frozen predictions. Values\n must be finite; Bernoulli y is 0/1. Fits once and releases transfer buffers.\ninfer {op:\"infer\"} -> {lower:float,upper:float}. Endpoints and their width must\n be finite and ordered, within [0,1] for Bernoulli. Repeated infer is identical.\n\nOne reset, one start, 1..8 chunks, one commit, two infer calls per episode.\nOnly one transaction per reset. Workload shape/outcome type is supplied by\nstart, never inferred from seeds. All responses <=64 KiB; complete JSON lines\nmust be <64 MiB. No paths, private targets, population labels, baseline outputs,\nextra labels, predictor retraining, or cross-episode state are permitted.\nConstant scores require label-only fallback. Matched score interventions and\none trailing reset replay are evaluator-owned controls, not self-report gates.\n\nCandidate: five-fold score-only zero/affine/spline controls with training-only\npaired-risk selection and offset-invariant variance. Constant/rare inputs\nretain the label-only fallback (Clopper-Pearson\nfor Bernoulli, Student t otherwise). Infer returns an asymptotic interval, NOT\nthe separate conservative bounded-fold certificate computed internally for\nBernoulli data. No certificate, fitted parameters or diagnostics are exposed.\nThe frozen predictor is never changed. Calibration uses other folds' labels\nand scores only, not the unlabeled score pool. See METHOD.md for assumptions,\noracle algebra, the scope of the certificate, and unperformed validation.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport base64\nimport math\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import beta, t\n\n\n_state = None\n\n\ndef _keys(value, expected):\n    if type(value) is not dict or set(value) != set(expected.split()):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid fields\")\n\n\ndef _label_only(outcomes, kind, alpha):\n    count = len(outcomes)\n    if kind == \"bernoulli\":\n        successes = int(outcomes.sum())\n        lower = 0.0 if successes == 0 else beta.ppf(alpha / 2, successes, count - successes + 1)\n        upper = 1.0 if successes == count else beta.ppf(1 - alpha / 2, successes + 1, count - successes)\n    else:\n        radius = t.ppf(1 - alpha / 2, count - 1) * outcomes.std(ddof=1) / math.sqrt(count)\n        lower, upper = outcomes.mean() - radius, outcomes.mean() + radius\n    return {\"lower\": float(lower), \"upper\": float(upper)}\n\n\nclass ScoreCalibrator:\n    \"\"\"Low-dimensional score nuisance; knots and ridge rule are prespecified.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, bounded):\n        self.bounded = bounded\n\n    def _features(self, scores):\n        scaled = np.clip(scores, -self.score_scale, self.score_scale) / self.score_scale\n        centered = np.clip(scaled - self.location, -3.0 * self.span, 3.0 * self.span)\n        standardized = centered / self.span\n        return np.column_stack([\n            standardized,\n            standardized ** 2,\n            np.maximum(standardized + 0.5, 0.0),\n            np.maximum(standardized, 0.0),\n            np.maximum(standardized - 0.5, 0.0),\n        ])\n\n    def fit(self, scores, outcomes):\n        self.score_scale = float(np.max(np.abs(scores))) or 1.0\n        scaled = scores / self.score_scale\n        lower, upper = np.quantile(scaled, [0.1, 0.9])\n        if upper <= lower:\n            lower, upper = float(scaled.min()), float(scaled.max())\n        self.location = 0.5 * lower + 0.5 * upper\n        self.span = float(0.5 * (upper - lower)) or 1.0\n        features = self._features(scores)\n        self.feature_mean = features.mean(axis=0)\n        self.feature_scale = features.std(axis=0)\n        self.feature_scale[self.feature_scale < 1e-8] = 1.0\n        design = (features - self.feature_mean) / self.feature_scale\n        self.outcome_mean = float(np.mean(outcomes))\n        centered_outcomes = outcomes - self.outcome_mean\n        penalty = np.diag([0.1, 2.0, 4.0, 4.0, 4.0])\n        self.coefficients = np.linalg.solve(\n            design.T @ design + penalty, design.T @ centered_outcomes\n        )\n        return self\n\n    def predict(self, scores):\n        features = (self._features(scores) - self.feature_mean) / self.feature_scale\n        prediction = self.outcome_mean + features @ self.coefficients\n        return np.clip(prediction, 0.0, 1.0) if self.bounded else prediction\n\n\nclass AffineCalibrator:\n    \"\"\"Training-only affine score calibration, with bounded outcome clipping.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, bounded):\n        self.bounded = bounded\n\n    def fit(self, scores, outcomes):\n        self.score_scale = float(np.max(np.abs(scores))) or 1.0\n        scaled = scores / self.score_scale\n        self.location = float(np.mean(scaled))\n        self.span = float(np.std(scaled)) or 1.0\n        standardized = (scaled - self.location) / self.span\n        self.outcome_mean = float(np.mean(outcomes))\n        denominator = float(standardized @ standardized)\n        self.slope = (float(standardized @ (outcomes - self.outcome_mean)) / denominator\n                      if denominator > 0.0 else 0.0)\n        return self\n\n    def predict(self, scores):\n        standardized = (scores / self.score_scale - self.location) / self.span\n        prediction = self.outcome_mean + self.slope * standardized\n        return np.clip(prediction, 0.0, 1.0) if self.bounded else prediction\n\n\nclass FittedControl:\n    \"\"\"Apply the fitted finite-N multiplier once; controls may be signed.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, calibrator=None, control_weight=0.0):\n        self.calibrator = calibrator\n        self.control_weight = float(control_weight)\n        self.lower_bound = min(0.0, self.control_weight)\n        self.upper_bound = max(0.0, self.control_weight)\n\n    def predict(self, scores):\n        if self.calibrator is None or self.control_weight == 0.0:\n            return np.zeros(scores.size)\n        return self.control_weight * self.calibrator.predict(scores)\n\n\ndef _fit_control(family, training_scores, training_y, sample_ratio, bounded):\n    if family == 'zero':\n        return FittedControl()\n    if family == 'affine':\n        calibrator = AffineCalibrator(bounded)\n    elif family == 'nonlinear':\n        calibrator = ScoreCalibrator(bounded)\n    else:\n        raise ValueError(\"unknown control family\")\n    calibrator.fit(training_scores, training_y)\n    training_control = calibrator.predict(training_scores)\n    weight_denominator = (1.0 + sample_ratio) * np.var(training_control, ddof=1)\n    control_scale = max(float(np.max(np.abs(training_control))), np.finfo(float).tiny)\n    if (not np.isfinite(weight_denominator)\n            or weight_denominator / control_scale / control_scale <= np.finfo(float).eps):\n        return FittedControl()\n    control_weight = np.cov(training_y, training_control, ddof=1)[0, 1] / weight_denominator\n    if not np.isfinite(control_weight):\n        return FittedControl()\n    return FittedControl(calibrator, control_weight)\n\n\ndef _select_control(training_scores, training_y, sample_ratio, bounded, rng):\n    \"\"\"Paired one-standard-error risk rule on identical inner validation folds.\"\"\"\n    inner_fold_count = min(3, training_y.size // 2)\n    inner_order = rng.permutation(training_y.size)\n    inner_folds = np.array_split(inner_order, inner_fold_count)\n    all_indices = np.arange(training_y.size)\n    family_risks = {}\n    for family in ('zero', 'affine', 'nonlinear'):\n        fold_risks = []\n        for validation_indices in inner_folds:\n            fitting_indices = np.setdiff1d(all_indices, validation_indices, assume_unique=True)\n            control = _fit_control(\n                family, training_scores[fitting_indices], training_y[fitting_indices],\n                sample_ratio, bounded,\n            )\n            validation_y = training_y[validation_indices]\n            validation_control = control.predict(training_scores[validation_indices])\n            risk_by_fold = np.var(validation_y - validation_control, ddof=1) + sample_ratio * np.var(validation_control, ddof=1)\n            fold_risks.append(risk_by_fold)\n        family_risks[family] = np.asarray(fold_risks)\n    selected_family = 'zero'\n    selected_fold_risks = family_risks[selected_family]\n    for family in ('affine', 'nonlinear'):\n        candidate_fold_risks = family_risks[family]\n        paired_excess = candidate_fold_risks - selected_fold_risks\n        risk_upper = np.mean(paired_excess) + np.std(paired_excess, ddof=1) / np.sqrt(paired_excess.size)\n        if risk_upper < 0.0:\n            selected_family = family\n            selected_fold_risks = candidate_fold_risks\n    return _fit_control(selected_family, training_scores, training_y, sample_ratio, bounded)\n\n\ndef bounded_mean_radius(sample_values, lower_bound, upper_bound, error_probability):\n    \"\"\"Two-sided empirical-Bernstein bound for an iid bounded mean.\"\"\"\n    if sample_values.size < 2 or not 0.0 < error_probability < 1.0:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid bounded-mean parameters\")\n    if (not np.isfinite(sample_values).all() or upper_bound < lower_bound\n            or np.any(sample_values < lower_bound) or np.any(sample_values > upper_bound)):\n        raise ValueError(\"values outside specified bounds\")\n    log_term = np.log(4.0 / error_probability)\n    radius = np.sqrt(2.0 * np.var(sample_values, ddof=1) * log_term / sample_values.size) + 7.0 * (upper_bound - lower_bound) * log_term / (3.0 * (sample_values.size - 1))\n    return min(float(radius), upper_bound - lower_bound)\n\n\ndef _control_statistics(labeled_y, unlabeled_scores, fold_controls):\n    \"\"\"Offset-invariant moments for fitted or independently fixed fold controls.\"\"\"\n    labeled_center = 0.0\n    labeled_variance = 0.0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '86,133p' results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/program.py; sed -n '215,370p' results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/program.py; cat results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/METHOD.md; ls task; find task -maxdepth 3 -type f | head; sed -n '1,90p' .codex/skills/retrieve-references/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '86,133p' results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/program.py; sed -n '215,370p' results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/program.py; cat results/ideas/02_offset_invariant_risk_adaptive_control_variates/program_1/src/METHOD.md; ls task; find task -maxdepth 3 -type f | head; sed -n '1,90p' .codex/skills/retrieve-references/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"        self.score_scale = float(np.max(np.abs(scores))) or 1.0\n        scaled = scores / self.score_scale\n        lower, upper = np.quantile(scaled, [0.1, 0.9])\n        if upper <= lower:\n            lower, upper = float(scaled.min()), float(scaled.max())\n        self.location = 0.5 * lower + 0.5 * upper\n        self.span = float(0.5 * (upper - lower)) or 1.0\n        features = self._features(scores)\n        self.feature_mean = features.mean(axis=0)\n        self.feature_scale = features.std(axis=0)\n        self.feature_scale[self.feature_scale < 1e-8] = 1.0\n        design = (features - self.feature_mean) / self.feature_scale\n        self.outcome_mean = float(np.mean(outcomes))\n        centered_outcomes = outcomes - self.outcome_mean\n        penalty = np.diag([0.1, 2.0, 4.0, 4.0, 4.0])\n        self.coefficients = np.linalg.solve(\n            design.T @ design + penalty, design.T @ centered_outcomes\n        )\n        return self\n\n    def predict(self, scores):\n        features = (self._features(scores) - self.feature_mean) / self.feature_scale\n        prediction = self.outcome_mean + features @ self.coefficients\n        return np.clip(prediction, 0.0, 1.0) if self.bounded else prediction\n\n\nclass AffineCalibrator:\n    \"\"\"Training-only affine score calibration, with bounded outcome clipping.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, bounded):\n        self.bounded = bounded\n\n    def fit(self, scores, outcomes):\n        self.score_scale = float(np.max(np.abs(scores))) or 1.0\n        scaled = scores / self.score_scale\n        self.location = float(np.mean(scaled))\n        self.span = float(np.std(scaled)) or 1.0\n        standardized = (scaled - self.location) / self.span\n        self.outcome_mean = float(np.mean(outcomes))\n        denominator = float(standardized @ standardized)\n        self.slope = (float(standardized @ (outcomes - self.outcome_mean)) / denominator\n                      if denominator > 0.0 else 0.0)\n        return self\n\n    def predict(self, scores):\n        standardized = (scores / self.score_scale - self.location) / self.span\n        prediction = self.outcome_mean + self.slope * standardized\n        return np.clip(prediction, 0.0, 1.0) if self.bounded else prediction\n\n\ndef _control_statistics(labeled_y, unlabeled_scores, fold_controls):\n    \"\"\"Offset-invariant moments for fitted or independently fixed fold controls.\"\"\"\n    labeled_center = 0.0\n    labeled_variance = 0.0\n    unlabeled_control = np.zeros(unlabeled_scores.size)\n    for heldout_indices, heldout_prediction, fold_unlabeled_prediction in fold_controls:\n        fold_residuals = labeled_y[heldout_indices] - heldout_prediction\n        fold_weight = heldout_indices.size / labeled_y.size\n        labeled_center += fold_weight * np.mean(fold_residuals)\n        fold_label_variance = fold_weight ** 2 * np.var(fold_residuals, ddof=1) / heldout_indices.size\n        labeled_variance += fold_label_variance\n        unlabeled_control += fold_weight * fold_unlabeled_prediction\n    estimate = labeled_center + np.mean(unlabeled_control)\n    variance = labeled_variance + np.var(unlabeled_control, ddof=1) / unlabeled_scores.size\n    return float(estimate), float(variance)\n\n\ndef _crossfit_controls(labeled_y, labeled_scores, unlabeled_scores, alpha, bounded, rng):\n    \"\"\"Fit only on training labels and scores; reuse the single unlabeled pool.\"\"\"\n    n_folds = min(5, labeled_y.size // 2)\n    order = rng.permutation(labeled_y.size)\n    all_indices = np.arange(labeled_y.size)\n    sample_ratio = labeled_y.size / unlabeled_scores.size\n    fold_controls = []\n    certified_radius = 0.0\n    fold_error_probability = alpha / (2.0 * n_folds)\n    for heldout_indices in np.array_split(order, n_folds):\n        training_indices = np.setdiff1d(all_indices, heldout_indices, assume_unique=True)\n        assert np.intersect1d(training_indices, heldout_indices).size == 0\n        control = _select_control(\n            labeled_scores[training_indices], labeled_y[training_indices],\n            sample_ratio, bounded, rng,\n        )\n        heldout_prediction = control.predict(labeled_scores[heldout_indices])\n        fold_unlabeled_prediction = control.predict(unlabeled_scores)\n        fold_controls.append((heldout_indices, heldout_prediction, fold_unlabeled_prediction))\n        fold_weight = heldout_indices.size / labeled_y.size\n        if bounded:\n            fold_residuals = labeled_y[heldout_indices] - heldout_prediction\n            residual_radius = bounded_mean_radius(\n                fold_residuals, -control.upper_bound, 1.0 - control.lower_bound,\n                fold_error_probability,\n            )\n            control_radius = bounded_mean_radius(\n                fold_unlabeled_prediction, control.lower_bound, control.upper_bound,\n                fold_error_probability,\n            )\n            certified_radius += fold_weight * (residual_radius + control_radius)\n    estimate, variance = _control_statistics(labeled_y, unlabeled_scores, fold_controls)\n    return estimate, variance, certified_radius\n\n\ndef _interval_from_statistics(center, variance, count, alpha, bounded):\n    radius = t.ppf(1 - alpha / 2, max(2, count - 6)) * math.sqrt(max(0.0, variance))\n    lower, upper = float(center - radius), float(center + radius)\n    if bounded:\n        lower, upper = float(np.clip(lower, 0.0, 1.0)), float(np.clip(upper, 0.0, 1.0))\n    return {\"lower\": lower, \"upper\": upper}\n\n\ndef _fit(values, settings):\n    count, unlab_count = settings[\"n\"], settings[\"N\"]\n    outcomes, predictions, unlab = np.split(values, [count, 2 * count])\n    if not np.isfinite(values).all() or len(unlab) != unlab_count:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid values\")\n    kind, alpha = settings[\"label_kind\"], settings[\"alpha\"]\n    if kind == \"bernoulli\" and not np.isin(outcomes, [0, 1]).all():\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid outcomes\")\n    label_only = np.all(unlab == unlab[0]) or np.all(predictions == predictions[0]) or (\n        kind == \"bernoulli\" and min(outcomes.sum(), count - outcomes.sum()) < 10\n    )\n    if label_only and kind != \"bernoulli\":\n        return _label_only(outcomes, kind, alpha)\n    center, variance, certified_radius = _crossfit_controls(\n        outcomes, predictions, unlab, alpha, kind == \"bernoulli\", _state[\"rng\"]\n    )\n    if kind == \"bernoulli\":\n        _state[\"certificate\"] = {\n            \"lower\": float(np.clip(center - certified_radius, 0.0, 1.0)),\n            \"upper\": float(np.clip(center + certified_radius, 0.0, 1.0)),\n        }\n    if label_only:\n        return _label_only(outcomes, kind, alpha)\n    return _interval_from_statistics(center, variance, count, alpha, kind == \"bernoulli\")\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    \"\"\"Dispatch the exact reset/update/infer protocol in the module docstring.\"\"\"\n    global _state\n    if type(request) is not dict:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid request\")\n    operation = request.get(\"op\")\n    if operation == \"reset\":\n        _keys(request, \"op seed\")\n        seed = request[\"seed\"]\n        if type(seed) is not int or not 0 <= seed < 2**63:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid seed\")\n        _state = {\"rng\": np.random.default_rng(seed), \"phase\": \"reset\"}\n        return {\"ok\": True}\n    if _state is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"reset required\")\n    if operation == \"infer\":\n        _keys(request, \"op\")\n        if _state[\"phase\"] != \"fitted\":\n            raise ValueError(\"commit required\")\n        return dict(_state[\"interval\"])\n    if operation != \"update\":\n        raise ValueError(\"unsupported operation\")\n    phase = request.get(\"phase\")\n    if phase == \"start\":\n        _keys(request, \"op phase n N alpha label_kind dtype encoding shape total_bytes chunks\")\n        count, unlab_count = request[\"n\"], request[\"N\"]\n        if any(type(value) is not int or value < 8 for value in (count, unlab_count)):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid shape\")\n        length = 8 * (2 * count + unlab_count)\n        if (_state[\"phase\"] != \"reset\" or length > 8 * 1024**2\n                or request[\"dtype\"] != \"<f8\" or request[\"encoding\"] != \"base64\"\n                or request[\"shape\"] != [2 * count + unlab_count]\n                or type(request[\"total_bytes\"]) is not int or request[\"total_bytes\"] != length\n                or type(request[\"chunks\"]) is not int or request[\"chunks\"] != math.ceil(length / 1024**2)\n                or type(request[\"alpha\"]) not in (int, float) or request[\"alpha\"] != 0.05\n                or request[\"label_kind\"] not in (\"bernoulli\", \"real\")):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid transfer\")\n        _state.update(phase=\"buffer\", settings=dict(request), buffer=bytearray(), next_index=0)\n        return {\"ok\": True, \"transfer_id\": \"buffer\"}\n    if _state[\"phase\"] != \"buffer\" or request.get(\"transfer_id\") != \"buffer\":\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid transfer state\")\n    if phase == \"chunk\":\n        _keys(request, \"op phase transfer_id index data\")\n        index, settings = request[\"index\"], _state[\"settings\"]\n        if type(index) is not int or index != _state[\"next_index\"] or index >= settings[\"chunks\"]:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid index\")\n        if type(request[\"data\"]) is not str or len(request[\"data\"]) > 1398104:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid base64\")\n        decoded = base64.b64decode(request[\"data\"], validate=True)\n        expected = min(1024**2, settings[\"total_bytes\"] - len(_state[\"buffer\"]))\n        if len(decoded) != expected:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid chunk length\")\n        _state[\"buffer\"].extend(decoded)\n        _state[\"next_index\"] += 1\n        return {\"ok\": True}\n    if phase == \"commit\":\n        _keys(request, \"op phase transfer_id\")\n        settings = _state[\"settings\"]\n        if len(_state[\"buffer\"]) != settings[\"total_bytes\"] or _state[\"next_index\"] != settings[\"chunks\"]:\n            raise ValueError(\"incomplete transfer\")\n        interval = _fit(np.frombuffer(_state[\"buffer\"], dtype=\"<f8\"), settings)\n        _state.pop(\"buffer\")\n        _state.pop(\"settings\")\n        _state.update(phase=\"fitted\", interval=interval)\n        return {\"ok\": True}\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid phase\")\n# Offset-invariant risk-adaptive controls: implementation note\n\nThis is a source-only candidate, not an evaluated research paper. No candidate\ncode, tests, simulations, package managers, network requests, or evaluators were\nrun during this stage. No measured coverage, width, score, or runtime is claimed.\nOnly the supplied task and permitted starter files were read; there was no\nliterature browsing or forbidden-content encounter. The larger paper/PDF,\nofficial template/checklist, real-data experiments, and repeated-run evidence\nremain unperformed, not replaced by illustrative numbers.\n\n## Interface, target, and scope\n\nThe target is the superpopulation mean theta = E[Y]. Inputs are labeled outcomes\ny[n], their frozen scalar predictions p[n], and independent unlabeled predictions\nu[N]. Assume iid labeled pairs and iid unlabeled scores from the same population,\nindependent of each other, with an externally frozen predictor. Conditioning on\none realized input fixes resources across methods; coverage still concerns\nrepeated sampling, not uncertainty conditional on all those realized values.\nInformative label selection, distribution shift, and predictor training on these\nlabels are outside this justification.\n\nThe reset/start/chunk/commit/infer protocol, validation, response keys/types,\ntransfer format, manifest and entrypoint are unchanged. Only infer returns\n`lower` and `upper`; no diagnostics or additional observations enter responses.\nReset clears fits and seeded random state. Training and selection use only outer\ntraining labels/scores and the supplied sample sizes; unlabeled values never\nenter training, selection, normalization, or multiplier fitting. No X, oracle\nlabels, baseline intervals, hidden inputs, or cross-episode memory are assumed.\n\n## Implemented selection and fitting\n\n`program.py::_crossfit_controls` partitions a reset-seeded permutation into\nK=min(5,floor(n/2)) held-out folds. Each has at least two observations for the\nsupported n>=8. Explicit index assertions verify the outer training boundary.\nEach outer training sample uses three inner folds (at least two validation\nobservations per fold at the smallest supported size). All families use exactly\nthe same inner splits, drawn before fitting any family.\n\nThe prespecified families, in increasing complexity, are:\n\n1. Zero control.\n2. Affine least-squares calibration of the scalar score. Score normalization,\n   intercept, and slope are fitted only on the relevant training sample. For\n   binary outcomes the calibration prediction is clipped to [0,1].\n3. The starter's small spline/ridge calibration: linear, quadratic and three\n   positive-part terms at normalized knots -0.5, 0 and 0.5. Training-only\n   10th/90th percentile scaling, range fallback, clipped features, and penalties\n   (0.1,2,4,4,4) are retained. Binary predictions are clipped to [0,1].\n\nFor each fitted nonzero calibration q, let rho=n/N using the full supplied\nsizes, even during inner fitting. Fit\n\n    b = Cov_train(Y,q(P)) / [(1+rho) Var_train(q(P))],\n    g(p) = b*q(p).\n\nNonfinite or machine-resolution-degenerate denominators, or nonfinite weights,\nproduce a zero control. The relative variance guard is measured against the\nsquared maximum training calibration magnitude. It is a numerical guard, not\na performance tuning parameter. The signed multiplier is applied exactly once;\nthere is no additional multiplication by N/(n+N) after fitting g. Binary q is\nclipped BEFORE multiplication, not after multiplying a potentially signed b.\n\nOn each inner validation fold compute\n\n    J_hat(g) = sample_var(Y-g(P)) + rho*sample_var(g(P)).\n\nStart from zero. Compare affine, then nonlinear to the currently selected\nfamily. Move only if the mean paired excess risk plus its foldwise sample\nstandard deviation divided by sqrt(number of inner folds) is negative. Refit\nboth calibration and multiplier on all outer training data after selection.\nValidation folds are equally weighted for this prespecified heuristic even\nwhen sizes differ by one. Because fits overlap, this foldwise quantity is NOT\na calibrated standard error, a significance test, or a coverage guarantee.\nNo completed intervals are compared or selected for their realized widths.\n\n## Offset-invariant shared-pool studentization\n\nFor fold k of size n_k and weight w_k=n_k/n, let g_k be its fitted control,\nr_ki=Y_i-g_k(P_i), and H(u)=sum_k w_k*g_k(u). The actual return path uses\n\n    theta_hat = sum_k w_k*mean(r_k) + mean(H(u)),\n    V_L = sum_k w_k^2*sample_var(r_k)/n_k,\n    V_U = sample_var(H(u))/N,\n    V_hat = V_L + V_U.\n\nVariance is computed only AFTER combining all controls on the same unlabeled\npool. This retains every covariance term from shared observations; a sum of\nfold-specific unlabeled variances would generally be wrong.\n\nReplacing each g_k by g_k+c_k changes r_k by -c_k and H by the constant\nsum_k w_k*c_k. Both the point estimate and variance are unchanged, including\nunequal fold sizes. The same center/variance implies identical endpoints under\nthe same interval builder. These shifted controls are abstract signed controls,\nnot modified predictor outputs; they must not be clipped again after shifting.\nPooled residual sample variance need not have this invariance. This algebra\nalone implies neither improved coverage nor a decrease in measured width.\nWithin-fold variance can be larger or smaller on individual realized samples.\n\n`_interval_from_statistics` retains the starter's Student-t critical value with\nmax(2,n-6) degrees of freedom. Binary endpoints are clipped to [0,1]. Constant\nscores in either pool and binary samples with fewer than ten successes or\nfailures retain the label-only rule: Clopper-Pearson for binary, Student-t for\nreal. Real Student-t intervals are exact under iid Gaussian outcomes, not\ngeneral real outcomes. The data-dependent binary fallback does not give the\ncombined Wald/fallback procedure an exact finite-sample coverage guarantee.\n\n## Variance identities and their limits\n\nFor deterministic fold controls, independent sampling gives exactly\n\n    E[theta_hat] = theta,\n    Var(theta_hat) = sum_k w_k^2*Var(Y-g_k(P))/n_k\n                    + Var(sum_k w_k*g_k(P))/N.\n\nThe labeled terms are independent because deterministic functions are applied\nto disjoint iid observations, and the unlabeled sample is independent of them.\nThe implemented sample-variance estimate is unbiased for this deterministic\nvariance, even when controls have different shapes. Normal or t coverage is\nstill not exact merely because variance is estimated without bias.\n\nFor a common deterministic control g, completing the square with\nm(P)=E[Y|P], e=E[Var(Y|P)], and a=N/(n+N) gives\n\n    V(g) = e/n + Var(m)/(n+N) + (1/n+1/N)*Var(g-a*m).\n\nFor an independently fixed calibration m_hat and J(g)=n*V(g), this yields\n\n    J(a*m_hat) = Var(Y) - a*Var(m) + a*Var(m_hat-m).\n\nThus calibration estimation error can consume the available prediction\nheadroom. At the oracle level, the optimal affine variance and nonlinear gap\nfor positive score variance are\n\n    V_affine = Var(Y)/n - N*Cov(Y,P)^2/[n*(n+N)*Var(P)],\n    V_affine - V_oracle = N/[n*(n+N)]\n                         *[Var(m)-Cov(Y,P)^2/Var(P)].\n\nThe gap is zero for affine conditional means, uninformative scores, and scores\nwith at most two support points. Estimated nonlinear fits are not guaranteed\nto improve on a fitted affine or zero control. These are deterministic oracle\nidentities, not a learned-nuisance coverage theorem or a universal lower bound\nover arbitrary confidence intervals.\n\nFor learned cross-fit controls, overlapping training folds create dependence:\nthe deterministic variance formula is NOT an exact conditional-on-training\nformula for the full estimator. A sufficient asymptotic framework is fixed K,\nn,N tending to infinity with N/n tending to a positive finite constant,\nfinite 2+delta moments with suitable uniform integrability for fitted residual\nand control squares, nondegenerate limiting variance, and stable selection and\nfitting so that g_k-c_k converges in L2 to one common deterministic g for every\nfold. The c_k may be training-dependent additive constants. They cancel exactly\nbefore the usual cross-fit argument. Conditional on the training observations\nfor a given fold, the centered held-out nuisance-error average is negligible\nat n^(-1/2) when its L2 norm tends to zero; the independent shared unlabeled\nsample is handled by the analogous average for the combined nuisance. Fixed\nK then gives the common-control first-order limit. The within-fold residual\nvariances and shared-pool variance consistently estimate its variance under\nthe stated moment/stability conditions, giving asymptotic Wald coverage.\n\nThese conditions need not hold for arbitrary unstable nuisance fits or\nnear-tied selections among distinct limiting controls. The one-standard-error\nrule does not establish them. A small basis need not converge to m. Heavy\ntails, rare binary events, unstable selection, and small samples require actual\ncoverage checks; there is no universal finite-sample t guarantee here.\n\n## Separate internal bounded certificate\n\nAs in the starter, bounded inputs also compute a standalone internal certificate\non every input, before choosing a fallback or Wald interval. If a fitted binary\ncontrol has weight b, it lies in [L,U]=[min(0,b),max(0,b)], since its calibration\nq lies in [0,1]. Its residual lies in [-U,1-L]. Bounds are training-only and\nremain valid for zero and signed controls. The original two-sided empirical\nBernstein radius is retained for each fold's residual and control means, with\nerror allocation alpha/(2K), weighted addition and a union bound. The sample\nvariance, range term and truncation are the starter's unchanged implementation.\nConditional independence within each held-out/unlabeled sample and a union\nbound suffice; no independence of reused pools or overlapping folds is claimed.\nThe weighted center is the same as the reported estimator, but this certificate\ndoes NOT determine the reported interval and is not exposed by RPC. The return\nmust not be described as certified.\n\n## Source-tree tests and deferred experiments\n\n`test_controls.py` contains executable unittest regression checks for the actual\n`_control_statistics` and `_interval_from_statistics` used in inference. They\ninject five unequal offsets AFTER fitting, into both held-out and shared-pool\ncontrols, for equal and unequal fold sizes, without reclipping. Other tests\ncheck the explicit shared-pool covariance formula, zero/constant controls,\nsingle-use finite-N weighting, identical inner splits and full-training refit,\nouter training-only inputs, small samples, finite bounded endpoints,\nconstant-score fallback, response keys, state transitions, and reset replay.\nThey were authored but NOT executed. When execution is authorized, from the\ncandidate directory the focused command is:\n\n    python -m unittest discover -s src -p 'test_controls.py'\n\nNo new dependency is introduced: implementation uses existing NumPy and SciPy;\ntests additionally use the Python standard library. Dependency versions and\nruntime have not been measured or pinned in this non-executing stage.\n\nRequired future scientific checks include paired repeated draws for incumbent,\nvariance-only, affine-only, selection-only and combined methods, plus label-only,\nPPI, tuned PPI++ and a permitted fixed-stratum comparator. Cover affine and\nnonlinear signals, weak/shuffled scores, rare events, heavy tails, unequal\nfolds, and varying N/n; use identical n,N and frozen predictions across methods.\nReport coverage uncertainty and paired width uncertainty. Real-table studies\nmust avoid distribution-shift pooling. The interface has no population labels\nor oracle means, so such diagnostics cannot be invented from seeds or presumed\nevaluator inputs. No empirical improvement, nominal coverage, completed paper,\nor publication artifact is asserted by this candidate.\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nevaluate_final.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\nresources\ntask.md\ntask_contract.json\n---\nname: retrieve-references\ndescription: Retrieve grounded BibTeX references from Semantic Scholar Graph API, OpenAlex, or Google Scholar (`scholarly`) and write references.bib plus provenance JSON for paper generation.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nFetch references from Semantic Scholar Graph API, OpenAlex, or Google Scholar (via `scholarly`) and keep only real BibTeX entries.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final report/paper generation.\n- Related-work section needs grounded citations.\n\n# What to do\n1. Build focused search queries from task goal, method names, and top experiment ideas.\n2. Preferred path (Semantic Scholar Graph API):\n```bash\npython scripts/s2_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n3. Fallback path (OpenAlex):\n```bash\npython scripts/openalex_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --search-limit 10 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n   Optional:\n   - `--mailto you@example.com` to enter the OpenAlex polite pool\n   - Env fallback: `OPENALEX_EMAIL`\n4. Alternate path (Google Scholar API wrapper with `scholarly`):\n```bash\npython -m pip install scholarly\npython scripts/scholarly_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --search-limit 25 \\\n  --sort-by relevance \\\n  --proxy-mode auto \\\n  --delay-seconds 0.5 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n   Proxy options:\n   - `--proxy-mode auto|none|free|single`\n   - `--proxy-http <url>` / `--proxy-https <url>` for `single`\n   - Env fallbacks: `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_MODE`, `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_HTTP`, `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_HTTPS`\n   - Global `HTTP_PROXY` / `HTTPS_PROXY` are intentionally ignored to avoid accidental ambient proxying\n5. Require `S2_API_KEY` when available for `s2_fetch.py` (script reads env automatically).\n6. Keep only references with valid BibTeX and citation key.\n7. Dedupe by citation key and paper id.\n8. Fail hard if references found `< min-refs`.\n9. Use `s2_fetch.py` first for stability, `openalex_fetch.py` as the next fallback, and `scholarly_fetch.py` last if the other sources fail.\n\n# Outputs\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n\n# Notes\n- Use `templates/provenance.schema.json` for expected shape.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
