{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '108,175p' source_evidence/METHOD.md; sed -n '185,232p' source_evidence/ppi.py; sed -n '1754,1812p' source_evidence/ppi.py; head -30 source_evidence/resource_manifest.json; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py | tail -60\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '108,175p' source_evidence/METHOD.md; sed -n '185,232p' source_evidence/ppi.py; sed -n '1754,1812p' source_evidence/ppi.py; head -30 source_evidence/resource_manifest.json; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py | tail -60\"","aggregated_output":"\nFor deterministic fold controls, independent sampling gives exactly\n\n    E[theta_hat] = theta,\n    Var(theta_hat) = sum_k w_k^2*Var(Y-g_k(P))/n_k\n                    + Var(sum_k w_k*g_k(P))/N.\n\nThe labeled terms are independent because deterministic functions are applied\nto disjoint iid observations, and the unlabeled sample is independent of them.\nThe implemented sample-variance estimate is unbiased for this deterministic\nvariance, even when controls have different shapes. Normal or t coverage is\nstill not exact merely because variance is estimated without bias.\n\nFor a common deterministic control g, completing the square with\nm(P)=E[Y|P], e=E[Var(Y|P)], and a=N/(n+N) gives\n\n    V(g) = e/n + Var(m)/(n+N) + (1/n+1/N)*Var(g-a*m).\n\nFor an independently fixed calibration m_hat and J(g)=n*V(g), this yields\n\n    J(a*m_hat) = Var(Y) - a*Var(m) + a*Var(m_hat-m).\n\nThus calibration estimation error can consume the available prediction\nheadroom. At the oracle level, the optimal affine variance and nonlinear gap\nfor positive score variance are\n\n    V_affine = Var(Y)/n - N*Cov(Y,P)^2/[n*(n+N)*Var(P)],\n    V_affine - V_oracle = N/[n*(n+N)]\n                         *[Var(m)-Cov(Y,P)^2/Var(P)].\n\nThe gap is zero for affine conditional means, uninformative scores, and scores\nwith at most two support points. Estimated nonlinear fits are not guaranteed\nto improve on a fitted affine or zero control. These are deterministic oracle\nidentities, not a learned-nuisance coverage theorem or a universal lower bound\nover arbitrary confidence intervals.\n\nFor learned cross-fit controls, overlapping training folds create dependence:\nthe deterministic variance formula is NOT an exact conditional-on-training\nformula for the full estimator. A sufficient asymptotic framework is fixed K,\nn,N tending to infinity with N/n tending to a positive finite constant,\nfinite 2+delta moments with suitable uniform integrability for fitted residual\nand control squares, nondegenerate limiting variance, and stable selection and\nfitting so that g_k-c_k converges in L2 to one common deterministic g for every\nfold. The c_k may be training-dependent additive constants. They cancel exactly\nbefore the usual cross-fit argument. Conditional on the training observations\nfor a given fold, the centered held-out nuisance-error average is negligible\nat n^(-1/2) when its L2 norm tends to zero; the independent shared unlabeled\nsample is handled by the analogous average for the combined nuisance. Fixed\nK then gives the common-control first-order limit. The within-fold residual\nvariances and shared-pool variance consistently estimate its variance under\nthe stated moment/stability conditions, giving asymptotic Wald coverage.\n\nThese conditions need not hold for arbitrary unstable nuisance fits or\nnear-tied selections among distinct limiting controls. The one-standard-error\nrule does not establish them. A small basis need not converge to m. Heavy\ntails, rare binary events, unstable selection, and small samples require actual\ncoverage checks; there is no universal finite-sample t guarantee here.\n\n## Separate internal bounded certificate\n\nAs in the starter, bounded inputs also compute a standalone internal certificate\non every input, before choosing a fallback or Wald interval. If a fitted binary\ncontrol has weight b, it lies in [L,U]=[min(0,b),max(0,b)], since its calibration\nq lies in [0,1]. Its residual lies in [-U,1-L]. Bounds are training-only and\nremain valid for zero and signed controls. The original two-sided empirical\nBernstein radius is retained for each fold's residual and control means, with\nerror allocation alpha/(2K), weighted addition and a union bound. The sample\nvariance, range term and truncation are the starter's unchanged implementation.\n        )\n        grads = w * (Y - ppi_pointest)\n        grads_hat = w * (Yhat - ppi_pointest)\n        grads_hat_unlabeled = w_unlabeled * (Yhat_unlabeled - ppi_pointest)\n        inv_hessian = np.eye(d)\n        lam = _calc_lam_glm(\n            grads,\n            grads_hat,\n            grads_hat_unlabeled,\n            inv_hessian,\n            coord=None,\n            clip=True,\n            optim_mode=lam_optim_mode,\n        )\n        return ppi_mean_ci(\n            Y,\n            Yhat,\n            Yhat_unlabeled,\n            alpha=alpha,\n            lam=lam,\n            coord=coord,\n            w=w,\n            w_unlabeled=w_unlabeled,\n        )\n\n    ppi_pointest = ppi_mean_pointestimate(\n        Y,\n        Yhat,\n        Yhat_unlabeled,\n        lam=lam,\n        coord=coord,\n        w=w,\n        w_unlabeled=w_unlabeled,\n    )\n\n    imputed_std = (w_unlabeled * (lam * Yhat_unlabeled)).std(0) / np.sqrt(N)\n    rectifier_std = (w * (Y - lam * Yhat)).std(0) / np.sqrt(n)\n\n    return _zconfint_generic(\n        ppi_pointest,\n        np.sqrt(imputed_std**2 + rectifier_std**2),\n        alpha,\n        alternative,\n    )\n\n\ndef ppi_mean_pval(\n    Y,\ndef _calc_lam_glm(\n    grads,\n    grads_hat,\n    grads_hat_unlabeled,\n    inv_hessian,\n    coord=None,\n    clip=False,\n    optim_mode=\"overall\",\n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the optimal value of lam for the prediction-powered confidence interval for GLMs.\n\n    Args:\n        grads (ndarray): Gradient of the loss function with respect to the parameter evaluated at the labeled data.\n        grads_hat (ndarray): Gradient of the loss function with respect to the model parameter evaluated using predictions on the labeled data.\n        grads_hat_unlabeled (ndarray): Gradient of the loss function with respect to the parameter evaluated using predictions on the unlabeled data.\n        inv_hessian (ndarray): Inverse of the Hessian of the loss function with respect to the parameter.\n        coord (int, optional): Coordinate for which to optimize `lam`, when `optim_mode=\"overall\"`.\n        If `None`, it optimizes the total variance over all coordinates. Must be in {1, ..., d} where d is the shape of the estimand.\n        clip (bool, optional): Whether to clip the value of lam to be non-negative. Defaults to `False`.\n        optim_mode (ndarray, optional): Mode for which to optimize `lam`, either `overall` or `element`.\n        If `overall`, it optimizes the total variance over all coordinates, and the function returns a scalar.\n        If `element`, it optimizes the variance for each coordinate separately, and the function returns a vector.\n\n\n    Returns:\n        float: Optimal value of `lam`. Lies in [0,1].\n    \"\"\"\n    grads = reshape_to_2d(grads)\n    grads_hat = reshape_to_2d(grads_hat)\n    grads_hat_unlabeled = reshape_to_2d(grads_hat_unlabeled)\n    n = grads.shape[0]\n    N = grads_hat_unlabeled.shape[0]\n    d = inv_hessian.shape[0]\n    if grads.shape[1] != d:\n        raise ValueError(\n            \"Dimension mismatch between the gradient and the inverse Hessian.\"\n        )\n\n    grads_cent = grads - grads.mean(axis=0)\n    grad_hat_cent = grads_hat - grads_hat.mean(axis=0)\n    cov_grads = (1 / n) * (\n        grads_cent.T @ grad_hat_cent + grad_hat_cent.T @ grads_cent\n    )\n\n    var_grads_hat = np.cov(\n        np.concatenate([grads_hat, grads_hat_unlabeled], axis=0).T\n    )\n    var_grads_hat = var_grads_hat.reshape(d, d)\n\n    vhat = inv_hessian if coord is None else inv_hessian[coord, :]\n    if optim_mode == \"overall\":\n        num = (\n            np.trace(vhat @ cov_grads @ vhat)\n            if coord is None\n            else vhat @ cov_grads @ vhat\n        )\n        denom = (\n            2 * (1 + (n / N)) * np.trace(vhat @ var_grads_hat @ vhat)\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"normalization\": \"ordered finite little-endian float64 columns; signed zero normalized before SHA256; real=(Y,Yhat), synthetic=(X,Y). No text/feature extraction.\",\n  \"seeds\": [\n    1000,\n    1001,\n    1002,\n    1003,\n    1004,\n    1005,\n    1006,\n    1007,\n    1008,\n    1009,\n    1010,\n    1011,\n    1012,\n    1013,\n    1014,\n    1015,\n    1016,\n    1017,\n    1018,\n    1019,\n    1020,\n    1021,\n    1022,\n    1023,\n    1024,\n    1025,\n        paragraph = _validate_conclusions_paragraph(raw_text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        print(f\"append_memory: rejected conclusions update — {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    conclusions_path = Path(args.conclusions_md)\n    history_path = Path(args.conclusions_history_jsonl)\n    prior = _read_prior_conclusions(conclusions_path)\n\n    trigger = str(args.gate_trigger).strip().lower()\n    record = {\n        \"timestamp\": utc_now(),\n        \"cycle_index\": int(args.cycle_index),\n        \"worker_index\": int(args.worker_index),\n        \"gate_trigger\": trigger,\n        \"gate_reason\": str(args.gate_reason).strip(),\n        \"prior\": prior,\n        \"new\": paragraph,\n    }\n    # History append must succeed before the live file changes so a crash\n    # mid-write can never lose the prior version.\n    _append_jsonl(history_path, record)\n    _atomic_overwrite(conclusions_path, paragraph + \"\\n\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef _run_append_event(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Execute the default event-append mode.\"\"\"\n    if not args.global_memory_md or not args.global_memory_jsonl:\n        print(\n            \"append_memory: --global-memory-md and --global-memory-jsonl are required \"\n            \"in event-append mode\",\n            file=sys.stderr,\n        )\n        return 1\n    event_file = Path(args.event_file) if isinstance(args.event_file, str) and args.event_file else None\n    event = _ensure_required_fields(\n        _load_event(event_json=args.event_json, event_file=event_file),\n    )\n\n    local_memory_path = Path(args.local_memory_md)\n    global_memory_md_path = Path(args.global_memory_md)\n    global_memory_jsonl_path = Path(args.global_memory_jsonl)\n\n    _append_text(local_memory_path, _markdown_entry(event), header=LOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS)\n    _append_text(global_memory_md_path, _markdown_entry(event), header=GLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER)\n    _append_jsonl(global_memory_jsonl_path, event)\n    return 0\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    \"\"\"Run the command-line entry point.\"\"\"\n    args = parse_args()\n    if args.write_conclusions:\n        return _run_write_conclusions(args)\n    return _run_append_event(args)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
